.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt BHYVE_CONFIG 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bhyve_config
.Nd bhyve configuration file format and variables
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr bhyve 8
uses a hierarchical tree of configuration variables to describe global and
per-device settings.
Internal nodes in this tree do not have a value,
only leaf nodes have values.
.Pp
These configuration variables can be assigned directly on the command line
or in a configuration file, one per line.
Besides variable assignments, a configuration file may contain special
directives, empty lines, or lines beginning with a
.Qq #
character, which are treated as comments.
.Pp
The only special directive currently supported is
.Sy include ,
which takes the path of another configuration file as argument, which is parsed
as any other configuration file.
The path to the configuration file to be included may be absolute, or relative
to the directory of the configuration file currently being processed.
.Pp
The values assigned to nodes are represented internally as strings and include
all whitespace, quotation marks, and other special characters specified between
the assignment operator and the next newline.
.Pp
Assignment takes one of the following forms:
.Bl -column "Operator" "config.node+=value" "Description"
.It Sy Operator Ta Sy Example Ta Sy Description
.It Vt = Ta Va config.node Ns Vt = Ns Va value Ta
The value is assigned to the specified node.
If the node already has a value assigned, the new value overwrites the existing
value.
.It Vt += Ta Va config.node Ns Vt += Ns Va value Ta
The value is assigned to the specified node.
If the node already has a value assigned, the new value is appended to the
existing value separated by a comma, allowing the incremental construction of
comma-separated lists.
.El
.Pp
The remainder of this manual describes the configuration variables understood by
.Xr bhyve 8 .
If additional variables are defined,
.Xr bhyve 8
will ignore them and will not emit errors for unknown variables.
However, these additional variables can be referenced by other
variables as described below.
.Sh VARIABLE VALUES
Configuration variable values are stored as strings.
A configuration variable value may refer to one or more other
configuration values by name.
Instances of the pattern
.Sq % Ns Pq Ar var
are replaced by the value of the configuration variable
.Va var .
To avoid unwanted expansion,
.Sq %
characters can be escaped by a leading
.Sq % .
For example,
if a configuration variable
.Va disk
uses the value
.Pa /dev/zvol/bhyve/%(name) ,
then the final value of the
.Va disk
variable will be set to the path of a ZFS volume whose name matches
the name of the virtual machine on the pool
.Pa bhyve .
.Pp
Some configuration variables may be interpreted as a boolean value.
For those variables the following case-insensitive values may be used to
indicate true:
.Pp
.Bl -bullet -offset indent -compact
.It
true
.It
on
.It
yes
.It
1
.El
.Pp
The following values may be used to indicate false:
.Pp
.Bl -bullet -offset indent -compact
.It
false
.It
off
.It
no
.It
0
.El
.Pp
Some configuration variables may be interperted as an integer.
For those variables,
any syntax supported by
.Xr strtol 3
may be used.
